WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.000
Kbo — The Namblong's Noken
Grime Valley, Jayapura, Papua, Indonesia

2
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:25.000
[In the Namblong language, the noken is called kbo —
a hand-knotted bag woven from natural fibers]

3
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:42.000
[Every tool, material, and movement in weaving
has a name in the Namblong language]

4
00:00:43.000 --> 00:01:00.000
[The craft is a bridge for maintaining the language
identity of the Namblong tribe]

5
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:20.000
["When I give you a noken, I'm offering more than a bag —
I'm sharing a piece of myself, a value"]

6
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:45.000
[Namblong's open-weave noken symbolizes
transparency and flexibility]

7
00:01:46.000 --> 00:02:10.000
[Ki — the Namblong word for woman — honors
the women who have kept this craft alive for generations]

8
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:30.000
[Used for daily life, ceremony, and symbolic exchange —
the noken carries both weight and meaning]

9
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:55.000
[UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage —
a tradition woven by hand, sustained by community]

10
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:28.000
[A thread from Papua —
still knotted, still carried forward]
